      "id": "hellmark-flathead",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to Ford's Flathead",
      "description": "Classic hop-up manual covering finned heads, multi-carb linkages, and cooling tips for Ford's 239 flathead V8.",
      "content": "Ford's 239 flathead V8 rewards patient hands and clean block passages. Scrub the water jackets thoroughly so both dual water pumps move coolant evenly, torque finned aluminum heads from the center studs outward, and re-torque them after the first heat cycle.\n\nSync progressive three-two-barrel carburetors so the center two-barrel carries crisp street response while the end carbs open together at full throttle, and set the front-mount distributor right on the timing mark.",
      "family": "ford-flathead",
      "parameter-name": "hp",
      "value": "3"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-sbc",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to Chevy's Small-Block",
      "description": "Shop manual covering valve lash, intake matching, and spark curves for GM's 265, 283, 327, and 350 small-block V8s.",
      "content": "GM's compact small-block V8 responds to careful assembly even before heavy machine work. Set rocker stud lash evenly across both banks with the engine warm so every valve opens to full cam lift without floating at the top of the quarter mile.\n\nMatch the intake manifold runners cleanly to the cylinder head ports, keep carburetor airflow close to 1.5 CFM per cubic inch across 265 through 350 cubic inches, and dial in spark advance with a timing light right on the balancer's sweet spot.",
      "family": "chevy-sbc",
      "parameter-name": "hp",
      "value": "5"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-yblock",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to Ford's Y-Block",
      "description": "Practical speed manual for oiling, rocker geometry, and tuning Ford's deep-skirt 292 Y-block V8.",
      "content": "Ford's 292 Y-block carries a rigid deep-skirt crankcase that handles hard launches when the top end is set up right. Flush the rocker-shaft oil passages thoroughly, set mechanical lash cold and re-check hot, and seal the valley cover snugly before bolting down the intake.\n\nBecause the Y-block's stacked intake ports turn sharply into the heads, careful gasket alignment and balanced carburetor linkage free up crisp midrange pull and steady top-end power.",
      "family": "ford-yblock",
      "parameter-name": "hp",
      "value": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-windsor",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to Ford's Windsor Small-Block",
      "description": "Street-and-strip tuning handbook for breathing, cam timing, and ignition setup on Ford's thin-wall 302 small-block V8.",
      "content": "Light, stiff, and eager to rev, the 302 Windsor thrives on clean airflow into and out of the heads. Torque the intake bolts in sequence from the center outward so the end seals stay oil-tight, and unshroud the air cleaner to feed a crisp four-barrel or multi-carb setup.\n\nWith the distributor mounted up front for easy reach, lock down the clamp only after checking full advance under the timing light and pair free-flowing headers with low-restriction mufflers to let the exhaust scavenge cleanly.",
      "family": "ford-windsor",
      "parameter-name": "hp",
      "value": "5"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-racing-slicks",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to racing slicks",
      "description": "Trackside handbook on tire pressures, burnout heat cycles, and compound care for maximum racing-slick traction.",
      "content": "Soft-compound racing slicks make their bite through heat and a flat contact patch. Drop cold tire pressures until chalk across the tread wears evenly from shoulder to shoulder, and bring the rubber up to temperature with a clean, straight burnout before staging.\n\nBrush grit and rubber marbles off the tread between runs, store spare wheels out of direct sun so the sidewall stays supple, and inspect wear closely so a thin carcass never lets go at top speed.",
      "product": "tires",
      "parameter-name": "grip",
      "value": "1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-gb-race5",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to the 5-speed racing",
      "description": "Close-ratio setup for the racing five-speed so the engine stays on its power through every shift.",
      "content": "The racing five-speed is a close-ratio box, and it pays only when the steps stay even. Fill it with light oil, set the shift forks so each dog locks without grinding, and keep the throw short enough that the engine never falls off its peak between gears.\n\nCheck the end play before the first hard pass. A tight cluster puts the crank's work on the pavement instead of heating the gear oil.",
      "product": "gb_race5",
      "parameter-name": "hp",
      "value": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-gb-man5",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to the 5-speed manual",
      "description": "Detent, fork, and overdrive notes so the five-speed manual wastes less of the engine's power.",
      "content": "Fifth on the five-speed manual is an overdrive, and the four gears under it still have to pull. Set the detent springs so the stick finds each gate, and keep the shift forks from riding the dogs once the gear is home.\n\nA clean 2-3 and a fifth that is not dragging let the engine make its power instead of stirring the gearbox.",
      "product": "gb_man5",
      "parameter-name": "hp",
      "value": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-gb-man4",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to the 4-speed manual",
      "description": "Linkage and clutch setup for the four-speed so the 1-2 shift does not give the power away.",
      "content": "The four-speed earns its keep on the 1-2 shift. Set the linkage so the stick travels straight across the gate, and set the clutch free play so the disc locks the moment the pedal comes up.\n\nEqual wear on the two center gears means the box is sharing the load. A box set up that way puts more of the engine's power to the rear wheels.",
      "product": "gb_man4",
      "parameter-name": "hp",
      "value": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-gb-man3",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to the 3-speed manual",
      "description": "Wide-ratio shift points and clutch care so the three-speed manual holds onto the power.",
      "content": "Three wide gears leave no room for a missed shift. Set second so the engine is still pulling when the stick comes back, and keep the clutch from slipping on the way out of low.\n\nFresh oil and a linkage that is not bent let this box pass the power along instead of turning it into heat.",
      "product": "gb_man3",
      "parameter-name": "hp",
      "value": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "hellmark-gb-auto3",
      "title": "Hellmark's guide to the automatic 3-speed",
      "description": "Band, fluid, and kickdown notes so the automatic three-speed stops slipping the power away.",
      "content": "An automatic only makes horsepower by slipping less of it. Set the bands to the shop's turns, keep the fluid at the mark and not burnt, and adjust the kickdown so the box drops a gear when the throttle asks instead of hunting.\n\nA converter that locks cleanly into each gear leaves more of the engine's work at the rear wheels.",
      "product": "gb_auto3",
